  "analyzed_vuln": {
    "function": "intersects",
    "file": "harfbuzz/src/hb-ot-layout-common.hh",
    "description": "`OT::Coverage::intersects` constructs `hb_auto_t<Coverage::Iter> iter(*this)`. The generic `hb_auto_t(T1 t1)` constructor takes its argument by value, so this call copies the complete C++ `Coverage` union before `Iter::init` examines its format. Sanitization only guarantees the format-dependent serialized minimum: for an unsupported Coverage format, `Coverage::sanitize` accepts the object after checking just the 2-byte format field. Consequently, a Coverage located near the end of the GSUB blob can be considered sanitized while lacking enough bytes for a full `Coverage` object; the implicit by-value copy then performs the reported 8-byte heap out-of-bounds read. The unsafe full-object copy in `Coverage::intersects`, rather than the iterator's later glyph access, is the root cause.",
    "conditions": "A crafted font must expose a GSUB contextual-substitution lookup (lookup type 5, Context format 2) that is reached during subset GSUB closure. Its Coverage offset must resolve near the end of the sanitized GSUB table, with at least the 2-byte Coverage format field in bounds but fewer than `sizeof(Coverage)` bytes remaining, and that format must be unsupported so `Coverage::sanitize` takes its permissive default branch. The PoC achieves this with overlapping/misaligned offsets inside the malformed lookup. `ContextFormat2::closure` calls `Coverage::intersects` for this object; construction of the iterator copies the undersized Coverage by value and reads past the table allocation before iteration can reject the unsupported format."
  },
